 In triangle $ABC$, $AB = BC$, so it's an isosceles triangle. Since, $\overline{BD}$ is the altitude, $BD$ is perpendicular to $AC$ and bisects it. Since, $\angle DBC$ and $\angle DBE$ are adjacent, $\angle DBE$ equals to $\angle DBC + 180^{\circ}$. Therefore, $\tan \angle DBE = -\tan \angle DBC$. By geometry of the problem, $\tan \angle CBE = 10$. $\frac{10}{-\tan \angle DBE}
  = \frac{10}{\tan \angle DBC}
  = \left(\frac{10}{\tan \angle DBC}\right)^2$
$\tan \angle DBE =  \sqrt{-10}$. $\cot \angle DBE = \cot (\tan \angle DBE) = \cot (\sqrt{-10})$. As $\cot \angle DBE$, $\cot \angle CBE$, $\cot \angle DBC$ form an arithmetic progression. So $\cot (\sqrt{-10}) - \cot (10) = \cot (10) - \cot (\cot (\angle DBE))$.  Follow through and by algebraic manipulation $\cot (\sqrt{-10}) = 2 \cot (10)$. $\frac{\cos\sqrt{-10}}{\sin\sqrt{-10}} = 2\cdot \frac{\cos 10}{\sin 10}$. $\cos\sqrt{-10} = 2 \cos 10. \sin \sqrt{-10} = \sin 10$ Let $\cos\sqrt{-10} = \sin 10 = t$. Using the identity $\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta = 1$. $\cos^2 \sqrt{-10} = 1 - \sin^2 10 = 1 - t^2 = \sin^2 \sqrt{-10}$. $t = \sqrt{1-t^2} $ or $t = -\sqrt{1-t^2}$. $t^2 = 1 - t^2$. $2t^2 = 1$. $t^2 = \frac{1}{2}$. $t = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$ or $t = -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$ Find $\sin 10$ by using $\sin (2\theta) = 2\sin\theta \cos \theta$. $\sin 20 = 2\sin 10 \cos 10 = 2\sin 10 \cdot\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \rightarrow \sin 10 = \frac{\sqrt{2}\sin 20}{2}$. Finally, $\boxed{ABC = \frac{1}{2} \times base \times height = \frac{1}{2} \times AC \times BE = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{sin 10^{\circ}} \times \frac{10}{cos10} =  \frac{6}{\sqrt{\sin 40^{\circ}}} }$</answer>

